BetterGuac on Arch Linux
========================

Install
  sudo pacman -U betterguac-1.6.0+bg10-2-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst     (or -aarch64)

  pacman pulls in Tomcat 10, MariaDB, a Java runtime and the guacd
  libraries. The install then:
    - initialises MariaDB in /var/lib/mysql if it has no data yet, with no
      anonymous accounts or test database, listening on 127.0.0.1 only
      (/etc/my.cnf.d/betterguac-local.cnf; delete it to open MariaDB up)
    - creates the betterguac databases and their accounts
    - converts the web application for Tomcat 10 (javax to jakarta) into
      /var/cache/betterguac
    - enables and starts mariadb, tomcat10 and betterguac.target
    - writes the first admin's email and password to
      /etc/betterguac/initial-admin.txt

  Then open http://<this machine>:8080/betterguac/ and sign in with the
  details in that file. The setup admin is not asked for MFA; every other
  admin enrolls on first sign-in. Delete initial-admin.txt once you have
  your own admin account.

Where things are
  Settings and keys ...... /etc/betterguac (guacamole.properties, sync.env,
                           betterguac.yaml, keys/, trusted-proxies)
  SSH keys ............... /etc/betterguac/keys, owned root:tomcat10, mode 640
                           (Arch's Tomcat runs as the tomcat10 user)
  guacd .................. betterguac-guacd.service, listens on 127.0.0.1:4822
  Web app ................ tomcat10.service, port 8080 (/etc/tomcat10/server.xml)
  Database backups ....... /var/lib/betterguac/backups (before each schema update)
  Licenses and sources ... /usr/share/betterguac/legal/

Tomcat's own example apps
  Arch's tomcat10 package also deploys Tomcat's docs, examples, manager and
  host-manager apps on port 8080. BetterGuac does not use them. If this
  machine only runs BetterGuac, add this attribute to the <Host> in
  /etc/tomcat10/server.xml:

    deployIgnore="^(docs|examples|manager|host-manager)$"

  so the line reads, for example:

    <Host name="localhost"  appBase="webapps"
          unpackWARs="true" autoDeploy="true"
          deployIgnore="^(docs|examples|manager|host-manager)$">

  then run: sudo systemctl restart tomcat10. Those paths then go to the
  BetterGuac sign-in page. Do not set deployOnStartup="false": Tomcat would
  then also skip BetterGuac, which is deployed from
  /etc/tomcat10/Catalina/localhost/, and every page returns 404.

Installed with 1.6.0+bg10-1?
  That build initialised MariaDB with Arch's defaults. If BetterGuac is the
  only user of this MariaDB, close it the same way:
    sudo mariadb -e "DELETE FROM mysql.global_priv WHERE user=''; DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS test; FLUSH PRIVILEGES;"
    printf '[mysqld]\nbind-address=127.0.0.1\n' | sudo tee /etc/my.cnf.d/betterguac-local.cnf
    sudo systemctl restart mariadb

Commands
  sudo betterguac-admin status
  sudo betterguac-admin migrate [--status]
  sudo betterguac-admin configure        re-run setup (safe to repeat)
  sudo betterguac-sync check|plan|apply /etc/betterguac/betterguac.yaml

Upgrade
  sudo pacman -U <newer package>. The database is backed up and updated
  automatically.

Remove
  sudo pacman -R betterguac removes the program files and keeps
  /etc/betterguac, /var/lib/betterguac and the databases. To remove those
  too, back up first, then:
    sudo mariadb -e "DROP DATABASE betterguac; DROP DATABASE betterguac_yamlsync;
      DROP USER 'betterguac'@'localhost'; DROP USER 'betterguac_sync'@'localhost';"
    sudo rm -rf /etc/betterguac /var/lib/betterguac

RHEL, Rocky, Alma and CentOS
  There is no RPM. Use the Docker image: bondjamesb07/betterguac.
